To terminate a C program cleanly, use:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    /* work */
    return 0;
}
```

or equivalently:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    /* work */
    exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);
}
```

Common options:

- `return 0;` — exits `main` successfully.
- `return 1;` or another nonzero value — signals an error to the operating system.
- `exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);` — terminates from anywhere, runs functions registered with `atexit`, and flushes/closes standard I/O streams.
- `exit(EXIT_FAILURE);` — terminates with failure status.
- `abort();` — immediately terminates abnormally, typically producing a crash/core dump; use for unrecoverable errors.
- `_Exit(status);` — exits immediately without running `atexit` handlers or flushing buffered I/O.

Example error handling:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    FILE *fp = fopen("data.txt", "r");

    if (fp == NULL) {
        perror("data.txt");
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    fclose(fp);
    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```
